WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.838
COLD MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.838 --> 00:00:05.380
I help some social savants with
their Datastore performance.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.992
Are we still on for
performance game night?

00:00:07.992 --> 00:00:08.950
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:15.850
FriendSet is building a
standard, run of the mill

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.110
mobile social media platform.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.761
However, as their user
loads started to rise,

00:00:20.761 --> 00:00:23.260
they noticed that the time it
takes to retrieve social posts

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.240
started to get
longer and longer.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.090
After a few hours of profiling
and working with their team,

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.420
we quickly saw the issue.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.619
Although their client was only
getting part of the fetch data,

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.410
their server was fetching
the full data per entity

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.830
from Datastore.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.520
And as the lists got larger,
their performance got worse.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.480
See, by default, Cloud Datastore
will return entire entities

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:44.860
as the result of a query.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.520
And most developers haven't yet
found that projection queries

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.594
can actually help them.

00:00:49.594 --> 00:00:51.760
We see a lot of implementations
with Datastore store

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.570
that looks something like this.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.810
A user makes a
request, and Datastore

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.310
fetches the relevant entities.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:00.610
Then some front end prunes
the entity properties

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.400
and passes on only
the relevant data back

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.959
to the requesting client.

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:07.482
Sadly, though, this is
a slow way to do things.

00:01:07.482 --> 00:01:09.190
I mean, you're fetching
the full entities

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.724
and then taking the
time to trim them down.

00:01:11.724 --> 00:01:13.390
For those situations
where you might not

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.880
need all of the properties
returned from an entity,

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.760
projection queries allows
Datastore to return only

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.370
what you need rather than
fetching everything and having

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.080
to do the culling yourself.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.550
For example, if
you need to access

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:29.140
only the keys from a query
result, use a keys-only query.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.210
This can be really
helpful in situations

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.480
where you need to do a keys-only
query first and then fetch

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.150
a subset of entities based
on the results from that

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.420
rather than executing
a larger general query.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.410
Likewise, using projections,
your query returns

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.180
abridged results with only
the specified properties

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.650
populated with values.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.360
Once again, since
the query is only

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.490
returning a subset of
data, you can end up

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.590
in a more performant position.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.820
Now, for FriendSet, we had
to do a few fancier things.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.370
But sadly, we don't have time
to cover all of that here.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.512
Instead, check out the
Datastore Queries page,

00:02:03.512 --> 00:02:05.470
where you'll find a whole
list of optimizations

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.050
for your queries that can
help in every situation

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:09.175
you might find yourself in.

00:02:09.175 --> 00:02:11.300
And don't forget to check
out the rest of the Cloud

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.699
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:14.240
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.450
every millisecond counts.

